// Every lane of a varying overwritten by a literal (t_afb4af65).
//
// The interesting half here is the COMPLETENESS GUARD, not the emission.
// The guard refuses a shader when a varying that feeds an output in the
// IR is read by no emitted instruction (t_72810bd7).  Here `color` does
// feed the output in the IR - through the insert chain - and is correctly
// read by nothing, because all four of its lanes are overwritten before
// the store.  The guard has to know the difference between work that was
// DROPPED and a value the source itself overwrote, or it refuses this
// shader for being correct.
//
// Reference compiler, for this shader:
//
//     FENCBR;
//     MOVR R0, {0x3f000000(0.5), 0x3e800000(0.25), 0x3e000000(0.125), 0x3f800000(1)};
//     # attributeInputMask None
//
// It reads no input at all, which is the same conclusion the guard has to
// reach.  Ours writes the four lanes as four MOVs rather than one and is
// not byte-identical here; the coalesced form is its own item.
void main(float4 color : TEXCOORD0,
          out float4 oColor : COLOR)
{
    float4 c = color;
    c.x = 0.5f;
    c.y = 0.25f;
    c.z = 0.125f;
    c.w = 1.0f;
    oColor = c;
}
